 # Linear Model Analysis for Plane Reproduction
-
 Also called: Geometric Initialization Justification, Theorem 1 Analysis
 
 ## Overview
-This analysis provides a theoretical justification for the loss function's ability to converge to the signed distance function (SDF) of a plane when the input data is sampled from a plane, using a linear model $f(x; w) = w^T x$ [S1, unofficial]. It sets apart from fixed volumetric grids (like RGVF) by being data-dependent and avoiding cubic-size memory requirements and interpolation schemes [S1, unofficial]. It proves that under specific conditions on the regularization parameter $\lambda$, gradient descent converges to the global minima corresponding to the plane's normal [S1, unofficial].
